<section id="about" class="hidden">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <h2>Thanks</h2>
    <p>This game was developed by the Open Data Institute based upon a tutorial written by <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/stephaniejyee/" target="_blank">Stephanie Yee</a> and <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/tonyhschu/" target="_blank">Tony Chu</a> which you can find at <a href="http://www.r2d3.us/" target="_blank">r2d3.us</a>.</p>
    <p>Housing data from R2D3, "A Visual Introduction to Machine Learning" by Stephanie Yee and Tony Chu (<a href="http://www.r2d3.us" target="_blank">http://www.r2d3.us</a>), licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" target="_blank">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/</a>). Selected, reduced to five features and arranged into card sets by the Open Data Institute.</p>
    <p>The housing data was collected for learning. It should not be used to draw conclusions about the New York or San Francisco property markets.</p>
    <h2>Customise your own version</h2>
    <p>If you wish to talk to us about developing a version of the game for your organisation then please <a href="mailto:bizdev@theodi.org?subject=Enquiry%20about%20machine%20learning%20game">get in touch</a>.</p>
    <h2>Report a bug/enhancement</h2>
    <p>You can report bugs via the Github issues page <a href="https://github.com/theodi/classification-game/issues" target="_blank">here</a>.</p>
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>A class result stores a team name, the decision tree, and the scores. A self-paced result is tied to the account email. Sign-in codes are deleted after they are used or they expire. The game does not store IP addresses. Results are deleted after 12 months.</p>
</section>